  "query_preview": "..\nThursday night through Monday: A closed upper level low \ncontinues to spin along the WA/OR coast, keeping the Inland \nNorthwest in an active pattern through Labor Day Weekend with \nperiodic showers and cooler than normal temperatures. A \nshortwave pivoting around the low Thursday evening into early \nFriday will bring a more widespread swath of precip across \nsoutheast WA and over the ID Panhandle. By later Friday morning,\nthis precip will wrap around into the higher terrain of \nnortheast WA and the North Cascades.\n\nShowers persist over the North Cascades through Friday afternoon\nand evening. Meanwhile, additional showers and thunderstorms are \nforecast to develop over southeast WA Friday afternoon as lapse\nrates steepen. The showers over the southeast will be more\nisolated in nature, while the showers over the North Cascades \nwill be more widespread. Saturday, Sunday, and Monday look to \nbring much needed precip regionwide with the entire area\nforecasted to receive measurable rainfall at some point during\nthis period.\n\nWinds will be lighter Friday than what we saw today, and\nafternoon highs will be in the 60s to lower 70s. Lows will be\nrather chilly Friday and Saturday mornings in the upper 30s to \nupper 40s. The coldest northern valleys may wake up to patchy \nfrost.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday: Models indicate the persistent upper \nlevel low will shift out of the region by the middle of next\nweek, allowing warmer and drier conditions to return. Expect\nhighs to gradually warm back into the mid 70s to low 80s over\nthe latter half of next week. /Fewkes",
